    <div class="msg-payload">
      
          <pre class="wordwrap">Hi Chenguang, Two things from running discovery documents and an MCP crawler, and a short answer to your last question. Protocol. &quot;MCP&quot; alone was not enough for us to talk to an endpoint. Since 2026-07-28, MCP servers start a session in one of two incompatible ways. Of the 21,474 remote servers from the official MCP registry that our crawler (HISTOR) has read since 25 October, 4,097 use the new 2026-07-28 way and 17,375 still need initialize. Our own client got the fallback wrong for three days and stopped reading about 2,000 servers it had read on 28 October. So I think 8.2 should say that the protocol comes with a version, or that the binding says how to find it. Freshness. A version number does not tell a client that a description has changed. Between 24 September and 20 October, 81% of the tool definition changes we recorded (17,401 of 21,401) kept the same serverInfo.version. The optional digest on the description endpoint does tell it. That may be worth a sentence next to Freshness. Your elements fit the discovery document in draft-artamokhov-dawn-marketplace-discovery: our &quot;name&quot; is your Display Name, &quot;catalogue_url&quot; is an Endpoint for continuing discovery, &quot;protocol_versions&quot; are its protocols, and &quot;generated_at&quot; is Freshness. The signing key is a Trust Reference outside the minimum, which is how we use it too. The data and queries behind these numbers: <a href="https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/mcp-protocol-eras-2026-11-01/" rel="nofollow">https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/mcp-protocol-eras-2026-11-01/</a> <a href="https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/mcp-protocol-eras-2026-11-01/" rel="nofollow">https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/mcp-protocol-eras-2026-11-01/</a> Best, Aleksandr
</pre>

      
    </div>
